WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.375
MCP tools should be treated like software integrations with permissions, owners,

00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:05.440
and risk ratings.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.760
A tool inventory should record what each tool does, who owns it, what server exposes it,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.135
what permissions it requires, what downstream systems it reaches, what data it can access,

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:16.200
and whether it is read-only or write-capable.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.990
A server inventory should capture provenance, installation source, maintainer,

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.040
dependencies, version, approval status,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.040
and whether the server is trusted for the current environment.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.560
Trusted and untrusted servers should not receive the same treatment.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.915
A locally reviewed internal server that exposes narrow read-only queries is different from

00:00:31.915 --> 00:00:34.600
an unknown server installed from an unreviewed source.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.380
Remote servers also introduce transport, authentication, tenant isolation,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.675
and service availability considerations.

00:00:40.675 --> 00:00:44.550
Local servers may introduce file access, command execution, process access,

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.405
or workstation data exposure.

00:00:46.405 --> 00:00:48.830
Both models can be useful, but both need governance.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.160
Tool descriptions are security-relevant metadata.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.718
They may guide the model's decision about when and how to call a tool.

00:00:54.718 --> 00:00:58.630
Descriptions should be reviewed for accuracy, scope, unsafe language, hidden assumptions,

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:02.058
and mismatch between advertised behavior and actual permissions.

00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:06.400
Allowlisting can limit which tools or operations are available in a given context.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.350
Version pinning and dependency review can reduce supply-chain drift.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.555
Disabling unused tools reduces the attack surface.

00:01:12.555 --> 00:01:14.440
Operational controls should match tool risk.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.200
Low-risk read-only lookup tools may need standard logging and authorization.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.170
Write-capable tools may need argument validation, approval gates, rate limits,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.345
transaction previews, dry-run modes, and rollback planning.

00:01:25.345 --> 00:01:29.325
Tools that touch local files, cloud resources, email, code repositories, tickets,

00:01:29.325 --> 00:01:32.000
or production systems deserve stronger review.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.640
The security habit is to ask what authority the tool carries,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.920
not just what friendly name appears in the client.
